---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { getPost } from "../../data/journal";

const post = getPost("camera-setup")!;
---

<Layout
  title={`${post.title} | EyesRhythm`}
  description={post.description}
  type="article"
  published={post.date}
  image={post.cover}
  imageAlt={post.coverAlt}
>
  <main id="main">
    <header class="cover">
      <img
        src="/images/blog-cover.jpg"
        alt="Someone sitting at a laptop. The camera at the top of the screen can see their face."
        width="1280"
        height="720"
      />
      <div class="cover__shade"></div>
      <div class="cover__copy">
        <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>{post.kicker}</p>
        <h1 class="display">{post.title}</h1>
        <p class="lede">{post.description}</p>
        <p class="meta">{post.read} · 27 August 2026</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="wrap article">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          EyesRhythm is a
          <a href="/blink-rate-detector">webcam blink rate detector</a>.
          It counts blinks through the camera already in your laptop.
          You do not need a special webcam. You just need the camera to see
          your face while you work.
        </p>
        <p>
          Video stays on this computer. Nothing is sent away. Follow the steps
          below once, then leave the app running.
        </p>

        <h2>1. Let EyesRhythm use the camera</h2>
        <p>
          The first time you open the app, your computer will ask for camera
          permission. Click <strong>Allow</strong>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Mac:</strong> System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security →
            Camera → turn EyesRhythm on.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Windows:</strong> Settings → Privacy &amp; security →
            Camera → allow desktop apps, then allow EyesRhythm.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>2. Close Zoom, Teams, or the browser camera first</h2>
        <p>
          Only one app can use the camera at a time. If a meeting is already
          using it, EyesRhythm will show a black picture, or it will not go
          Live. Close those apps, then open EyesRhythm.
        </p>

        <h2>3. Sit the way you normally sit</h2>
        <p>
          Put the laptop or monitor so the camera is near your eye line. Sit
          about an arm’s length away, the same as a normal desk.
        </p>
        <p>
          Too close, and your face fills the picture. Too far, and your eyes
          look tiny. Look at the screen, not down at the keyboard for a long
          time, if you want this stretch of work to count.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you look clearly down, or turn far to the side, the app treats
          that as <strong>off-screen</strong>. That is on purpose. The score is
          about time at the screen, not the whole room.
        </p>

        <h2>4. Put the light in front of you</h2>
        <p>
          Face a window or a lamp. Do not sit with a bright window behind your
          head. That turns your face dark, and blinks are hard to see.
        </p>
        <p>
          Glasses are fine. A little extra light on your eyes helps. You do
          not need a ring light.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Look at the screen, not past it</h2>
        <p>
          The app needs to see both eyes, looking mostly toward the camera. A
          quick glance away is fine. Working with your head turned to the side
          is not, those blinks will not count.
        </p>

        <h2>6. Check once, then hide the camera</h2>
        <p>
          In the app, choose <strong>Lens</strong> for a few seconds. You
          should see your face, both eyes, and a Live light. Then switch back
          to Atlas or another face. You do not need to watch yourself all day.
        </p>
        <p>
          While you look at the screen, the status should say the eyes are
          open. If it stays off-screen, sit up, raise the laptop, or add
          light.
        </p>

        <h2>If you use more than one screen</h2>
        <p>
          Extra monitors are fine. The camera still has to see your face. How
          you place the laptop matters.
        </p>

        <h3>Best: laptop in the middle</h3>
        <p>
          Keep the laptop in the centre, facing you. Put one or two screens on
          the sides. Look mostly toward the laptop so its built-in camera can
          see your eyes. A glance to the side is fine. Sitting turned toward a
          side screen for a long time is not.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/setup-center.jpg"
            alt="A laptop in the centre of the desk with a monitor on each side."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Laptop in the centre. Side screens angle in. The webcam looks at
            your face.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>If the laptop is off to the side</h3>
        <p>
          If you look at other screens and the laptop sits to the left or
          right, the built-in camera is looking at your cheek, not your eyes.
          Blink counts will be poor.
        </p>
        <p>
          In that case use a small external webcam on the screen in front of
          you. Point it at your face. Keep the angle from your face to that
          camera as small as you can, almost straight on, not from the side
          or from far above.
        </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src="/images/setup-side.jpg"
            alt="Two monitors in front with an external webcam on top, laptop closed to the side."
            width="1280"
            height="720"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Laptop parked to the side. Webcam on the screen you actually look
            at.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Quick check</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Camera permission is on.</li>
          <li>No other app is using the camera.</li>
          <li>Camera near eye height, normal desk distance.</li>
          <li>Light on your face, not a window behind you.</li>
          <li>Lens shows your face, then you hide the preview.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>If it is not working</h2>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>What you see</th>
              <th>What to try</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Black camera, not Live</td>
              <td>
                Close Zoom, Teams, or any browser tab using the camera. Check
                camera permission again.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Always off-screen</td>
              <td>
                Sit up. Raise the screen. Look at the display, not down at the
                desk.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Blinks missed with glasses</td>
              <td>Face the camera and add a bit of light on your eyes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>The number jumps around</td>
              <td>
                Stay in the picture for a minute. Looking away pauses the
                score on purpose.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
          After this, leave it. EyesRhythm is a small window next to your work,
          not another chore.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="back">
        <a href="/journal">All journal</a>
        ·
        <a href="/blink-rate-detector">Blink rate detector</a>
        ·
        <a href="/journal/count-is-not-rhythm">Why the blink count is not enough</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .cover {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: min(72vh, 640px);
    display: grid;
    align-items: end;
    color: #fff;
    background: #1d1d1f;
  }

  .cover img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .cover__shade {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15) 20%,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.62) 100%
    );
  }

  .cover__copy {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    width: min(var(--wrap), calc(100% - var(--gutter) * 2));
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 48px 0 40px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .cover .kicker {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86);
  }

  .cover .display {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.2rem);
    max-width: 14ch;
    color: #fff;
  }

  .cover .lede {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.88);
    max-width: 34rem;
  }

  .meta {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7);
  }

  .article {
    padding: 48px 0 96px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .shot {
    margin: 16px 0 4px;
    width: min(100%, 42rem);
  }

  .shot img {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: 20px;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .shot figcaption {
    margin-top: 8px;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .back {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .back button {
    color: var(--blue-deep);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
